---
import SabGuide from '../../components/sab/SabGuide.astro';
import { getGuide } from '../../lib/guides';

const guide = getGuide('trades-scholarships-rap-alberta');
---

<SabGuide guide={guide}>
  <p>
    Scholarship advice in Alberta is written almost entirely for university-bound students, which is strange, because trades students have some of the best-funded and least-competitive money in the province. If you're heading into an apprenticeship — or already earning hours through RAP — this is your map.
  </p>

  <h2>RAP in one section</h2>
  <p>
    The Registered Apprenticeship Program lets Alberta high school students work as paid apprentices while finishing their diploma. You earn a wage, you earn high school credits for your work hours, and those same hours count toward the first period of your apprenticeship after graduation. You leave Grade 12 with a diploma, a head start on your trade, and money in the bank — while your classmates are writing tuition cheques.
  </p>
  <p>
    Getting in goes through your school: talk to your off-campus education coordinator or guidance counsellor, who connects you with an employer willing to take a registered apprentice. If your school runs trades-related CTS courses, that's the natural on-ramp.
  </p>

  <h2>The scholarships attached to RAP</h2>
  <p>
    This is the part almost nobody knows: doing RAP makes you eligible for dedicated scholarships with tiny applicant pools.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>High School Apprenticeship Scholarship — $1,000.</strong> For Grade 12 students registered as apprentices who plan to continue in their trade. This is the successor to the long-running RAP scholarship, and it exists specifically to reward students like you.</li>
    <li><strong>High School Apprenticeship Scholarship: Bright Futures — $2,000.</strong> The bigger sibling, aimed at RAP students continuing their apprenticeship after graduation.</li>
    <li><strong>Skills Canada Alberta awards.</strong> Competing at Skills Canada regionals or provincials opens its own scholarship tier, up to the $20,000 Terry Cooke Legacy Scholarship. If your shop teacher has ever suggested entering, that's the reason.</li>
    <li><strong>Corporate trades awards.</strong> Energy and construction companies fund trades-specific scholarships — TC Energy's STEM &amp; Trades awards are the well-known example — because they are recruiting you, not doing you a favour.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The current crop, with live deadlines, is under the <a href="/scholarships/?category=Trades">Trades filter in the directory</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>One caution about the Rutherford</h2>
  <p>
    The <a href="/guides/alexander-rutherford-scholarship-guide/">Alexander Rutherford Scholarship</a> requires full-time enrolment in a post-secondary program at least one semester long. A pre-employment trades certificate or diploma at a college qualifies; the short technical-training blocks inside a standard apprenticeship usually don't. If you're apprenticing, check your program against Alberta Student Aid's rules before counting on that money — and if you're doing a college trades program first, apply, because your Grade 12 marks are worth up to $2,500 either way.
  </p>

  <h2>Why the odds are so good</h2>
  <p>
    Most scholarship applicants are chasing the same university-track awards, so trades-specific money goes under-applied year after year. And local awards from <a href="/guides/local-scholarships-better-odds/">service clubs and community foundations</a> read a RAP placement exactly the way they read a varsity captaincy: as proof you show up and work. A paying apprenticeship is not a weaker application story than a student council seat — to most committees it's a stronger one.
  </p>
  <p>
    The <a href="/match/">match quiz</a> asks about apprenticeship status directly, so two minutes there will surface everything you qualify for.
  </p>
</SabGuide>
